{"lines":[{"w":0.072710876464843754,"h":0.01460152660859626,"x":0.83506910781897659,"t":"Reading","c":1,"y":0.11285493920798695},{"y":0.15547703169251803,"w":0.26748970031738284,"c":1,"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","h":0.013250883392226132,"x":0.17901235138451849},{"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading","h":0.017207948844199739,"x":0.17896691860202035,"y":0.18589521776378504,"w":0.72027252197265623,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"Passage 3 below.","y":0.20347467623478044,"h":0.01309509131315878,"x":0.18106995793647485,"w":0.14403292338053386},{"h":0.020442997468514745,"y":0.23691860426481304,"x":0.42592593371797044,"w":0.23251027425130205,"c":1,"t":"The power of play"},{"h":0.015769408645000871,"t":"Virtually every child, the world over, plays. The drive to play is so intense that children will do so","c":1,"x":0.18101003418884251,"y":0.27077936558035409,"w":0.72233647664388023},{"t":"in any circumstances, for instance when they have no real toys, or when parents do not actively","w":0.70585545857747412,"h":0.0163954430390304,"x":0.17896605587214698,"y":0.28629937991690557,"c":1},{"w":0.70793650309244793,"c":1,"y":0.3019130237297809,"t":"encourage the behavior. In the eyes of a young child, running, pretending, and building are fun.","x":0.17895281160769702,"h":0.016567138114722546},{"c":1,"x":0.17695473063590092,"w":0.67901234944661448,"t":"Researchers and educators know that these playful activities benefit the development of the","y":0.31831395335300683,"h":0.014534883982160851},{"t":"whole child across social, cognitive, physical, and emotional domains. Indeed, play is such an","c":1,"w":0.69765182495117184,"x":0.17690176531296137,"h":0.017768362245233926,"y":0.33264201695662343},{"y":0.34883720915631133,"w":0.72222223917643236,"h":0.016003780005537038,"t":"instrumental component to healthy child development that the United Nations High Commission","x":0.17695472392759484,"c":1},{"w":0.594826151529948,"y":0.36328635078418792,"h":0.018607921398149374,"t":"on Human Rights (1989) recognized play as a fundamental right of every child.","x":0.17685786117770191,"c":1},{"x":0.18106996785802618,"w":0.66255142211914064,"t":"Yet, while experts continue to expound a powerful argument for the importance of play in","h":0.013081395583944722,"y":0.39534883708950086,"c":1},{"h":0.017679161682847888,"c":1,"y":0.40966574896825059,"x":0.17690007453308937,"t":"children's lives, the actual time children spend playing continues to decrease. Today, children play","w":0.72850336710611985},{"y":0.42579505309295174,"t":"eight hours less each week than their counterparts did two decades ago (Elkind 2008). Under","w":0.69341562906901033,"x":0.17901234997040139,"h":0.013250883392226021,"c":1},{"t":"pressure of rising academic standards, play is being replaced by test preparation in kindergartens","c":1,"w":0.72235392252604169,"h":0.017590311587348251,"y":0.44020015438089866,"x":0.17688066467526387},{"x":0.17901234275042999,"y":0.45784883708965896,"w":0.68518519083658846,"h":0.013081395583944611,"c":1,"t":"and grade schools, and parents who aim to give their preschoolers a leg up are led to believe"},{"h":0.01833368105938904,"c":1,"x":0.17892367227709882,"t":"that flashcards and educational 'toys' are the path to success. Our society has created a false","w":0.68329203287760421,"y":0.46989213643082794},{"c":1,"t":"dichotomy between play and learning.","x":0.17680867668367806,"y":0.48830129083356932,"h":0.015870020162090381,"w":0.28836095174153648},{"x":0.1789627861514157,"h":0.016947075671947531,"t":"Through play, children learn to regulate their behavior, lay the foundations for later learning in","y":0.5174024981967088,"w":0.7038004048665365,"c":1},{"t":"science and mathematics, figure out the complex negotiations of social relationships, build a","w":0.68737614949544268,"x":0.17894750686950905,"h":0.016481480413106708,"y":0.53273973434082611,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.17894720033721551,"h":0.015258108347970256,"t":"repertoire of creative problem-solving skills, and so much more. There is also an important role","w":0.71206130981445304,"y":0.54881700319163484},{"x":0.17695473868245776,"h":0.014570836043610647,"c":1,"t":"for adults in guiding children through playful learning opportunities.","y":0.56537102454909283,"w":0.51440327962239585},{"y":0.59432642699170746,"w":0.72642389933268237,"c":1,"h":0.015423301251954014,"x":0.17896721531974605,"t":"Full consensus on a formal definition of play continues to elude the researchers and theorists who"},{"t":"study it. Definitions range from discrete descriptions of various types of play such as physical,","h":0.020038772106732328,"w":0.70388315836588544,"c":1,"y":0.60790245309779811,"x":0.17684832921999752},{"y":0.62496744010237137,"w":0.72032099405924477,"x":0.1768859792884416,"c":1,"t":"construction, language, or symbolic play (Miller & Almon 2009), to lists of broad criteria, based","h":0.017572099665449703},{"w":0.69351552327473964,"x":0.17895982056936893,"h":0.016232489416259432,"t":"on observations and attitudes, that are meant to capture the essence of all play behaviors (e.g.","y":0.64044343176424057,"c":1},{"h":0.01308995562532056,"c":1,"x":0.17695473162095474,"t":"Rubin et al. 1983).","y":0.6584216723894476,"w":0.14403292338053383},{"w":0.71214797973632793,"y":0.68600591246092701,"h":0.018123832271292772,"c":1,"x":0.17891931163211866,"t":"A majority of the contemporary definitions of play focus on several key criteria. The founder of"},{"y":0.70200235586142634,"t":"the National Institute for Play, Stuart Brown, has described play as 'anything that spontaneously","w":0.71399175008138027,"x":0.1769547423828115,"c":1,"h":0.016020900088288936},{"x":0.17901234916628178,"w":0.69958847045898431,"h":0.014723203769140158,"y":0.71790341565873284,"t":"is done for its own sake'. More specifically, he says it 'appears purposeless, produces pleasure","c":1},{"c":1,"w":0.73251027425130188,"x":0.17695473942899004,"y":0.73401162777187545,"h":0.01453488398216074,"t":"and joy, [and] leads one to the next stage of mastery' (as quoted in Tippett 2008). Similarly, Miller"},{"w":0.73060363769531245,"t":"and Almon (2009) say that play includes 'activities that are freely chosen and directed by children","h":0.018253050648000646,"y":0.74709315938102516,"c":1,"x":0.17687205086771826},{"y":0.76442873987177384,"h":0.013250883392226132,"c":1,"x":0.17901235000824745,"t":"and arise from intrinsic motivation'. Often, play is defined along a continuum as more or less","w":0.69341562906901044},{"w":0.70384226481119794,"h":0.017697992538254437,"y":0.77876133418023319,"c":1,"t":"playful using the following set of behavioral and dispositional criteria (e.g. Rubin et al. 1983):","x":0.17688083988599998},{"w":0.63389439900716149,"t":"Play is pleasurable: Children must enjoy the activity or it is not play. It is intrinsically","x":0.22009187659643981,"y":0.81023911245699298,"h":0.016714913825404687,"c":1},{"h":0.017648181308705935,"w":0.65446629842122395,"t":"motivated: Children engage in play simply for the satisfaction the behavior itself brings.","x":0.22008170819862224,"y":0.82477735124991203,"c":1},{"w":0.66460906982421875,"h":0.01453488398216074,"x":0.22222221398043548,"c":1,"y":0.84156976730824218,"t":"It has no extrinsically motivated function or goal. Play is process oriented: When children"},{"c":1,"x":0.88271604949522875,"y":0.86918604635428165,"w":0.024691357612609877,"t":"67","h":0.011627907185728592}],"h":3395,"w":2400}